  "material": "White PLA",
  "notes": "Headpiece scaled so the skull is 150 mm wide (about 24 cm long), about the width of a 58 cm head as in the reference photo; worn with the snout tipped forward over the brow and cords through the eye sockets. The horns (about 20 cm) print separately; each has a seat shaped exactly like the skull where it attaches, so it only fits one way. Glue with epoxy or thick superglue. The skull prints upright on its underside (the side against the head), so support marks stay hidden. Skull and horns: 3 walls, 10 % gyroid infill to keep the headpiece light. Spine, arm pieces (59 × 42 mm, 8×) and ribs keep the smaller body scale; spine and arm pieces print flat side down, cut flat by up to 1 mm where that side is domed. Ribs: 3 left + 3 mirrored right, 220 mm long (the full scale, ~290 mm, doesn't fit the printer). Plates: skull (~11.6 h) and horns (~10.4 h) are night plates and can run the same night on the two printers; spine + arm pieces (~8.6 h) is the next night; ribs (~6 h) need the full bed width, so they get a daytime plate. All plates use tree supports and a small brim.",
